WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.280
Captain Marvel blasted into theaters over
its opening weekend with an astonishing domestic

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.870
haul of over $150 million in ticket sales.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:14.389
Adding global receipts bumped that figure
to a staggering $455 million, making it the

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:17.420
sixth-highest global debut of all time.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:21.179
While those numbers are impressive, when you
step back and consider why the film did so

00:00:21.179 --> 00:00:23.949
well, it all comes into focus pretty quickly.

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:28.910
From its impact on larger MCU storylines to
some background on who Captain Marvel actually

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:33.530
is, here are some of the top reasons that
Carol Danvers' debut flew away with the box

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:34.280
office.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:35.640
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.620
The elephant in the room for all things Marvel
right now is Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:44.710
Coming off last spring's anxiety-inducing
third Avengers installment, at least half

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.780
of the population is dying to see what happens
next.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.420
The pent-up demand for Endgame has lent a
boost to the Marvel movies that came out in

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.730
the interim.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:58.449
Ant-Man and the Wasp pulled in a neat $617
million worldwide and Captain Marvel has made

00:00:58.449 --> 00:01:02.000
over two thirds that total in its first few
days of release.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.950
While there are a ton of different factors
for Captain Marvel's success, the backdrop

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:10.390
of Endgame looming a mere seven weeks after
its release date surely helped the movie soar

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:11.620
to blockbuster heights.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.820
While any Marvel movie is sure to have the
answers to a lot of questions, Captain Marvel

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:18.620
was absolutely full of them.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.700
Did it answer them?

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.700
"That's a yes or no question."

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:22.680
"Yes."

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.060
Well, sort of.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:28.120
There were internal questions like who Jude
Law was playing or how the filmmakers would

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.229
choose to represent the Supreme Intelligence
of the Kree.

00:01:31.229 --> 00:01:35.470
There were also some answers to bigger questions,
like how the Tesseract ended up where it did

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:39.540
in the beginning of the first Avengers film
or how Carol Danvers would make her entrance

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:40.770
in Endgame.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:44.770
The question of how Nick Fury's pager could
reach someone lightyears away was answered

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:49.130
when Carol upgraded it to be able to reach
a distance of "a couple galaxies" in the closing

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:50.690
minutes of Captain Marvel.

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.480
So why didn't Fury didn't use it until Infinity
War?

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.520
Carol asked that it only be used for emergencies
only.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.820
Many of the lead characters that have found
their way into the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:04.990
have been at least semi-familiar to audiences
before they hit the big screen.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:09.680
Hulk, Captain America, and Spider-Man were
well-established mainstream names even before

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.440
their movies were released.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.660
Captain Marvel, on the other hand, was about
a character who was, in many ways, a mystery,

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.290
even to herself.

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:21.520
Before the symbol popped up on Fury's pager,
most fans who weren't particularly acquainted

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.680
with deeper comic book lore might have been
a bit stumped as to why the device had anything

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.130
to do with stopping Thanos.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:31.330
Of course, the question only added fuel to
the fire, giving Carol Danvers a mystique

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.640
that was a key driver in getting curious fans
to see the film.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.500
The success of shows like Stranger Things
has continued to confirm that nostalgia-driven

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:44.190
pop culture can be big business, and Captain
Marvel takes full advantage.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:48.260
Pretty much the entire film is set during
the 1990s, and the screenplay capitalized

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.450
on the opportunity to throw out as many mid-'90s
callbacks as possible.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:56.420
From the moment she arrives on Earth by crashing
through a Blockbuster Video store's rooftop,

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.920
the '90s references are hot and heavy.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.130
A security guard directs Danvers to a Radio
Shack, where she uses a Game Boy to wire the

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.690
phone for intergalactic capabilities.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:09.730
In a cafe, Danvers tries to use Alta Vista
to search for information.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:13.940
Through the whole movie, the satisfying nostalgia-fest
just keeps going from start to finish.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.400
While Captain Marvel had more than enough
firepower to succeed on its own, it did have

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.070
help, and not just because it's a sort of
opening act for Endgame.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:27.420
Just as importantly, it arrived at the end
of a subpar winter for the movies in general.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:33.280
While 2018 was a huge year for theaters across
the globe, a couple of months into 2019 the

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.160
numbers appear anything but grand by comparison.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:42.590
The U.S. box office brought in a measly 1.2
billion between January 1 and February 18,

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:45.110
marking its lowest start to the year since
2011.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:49.810
However, once Captain Marvel swooped into
theaters, moviegoers flocked to see the first

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.810
new MCU addition of the year.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:56.530
It added up to an insane amount of cash over
those first few days after its opening, nearly

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:00.060
a third of the total generated during the
first seven weeks of the year.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:05.360
Marvel has historically been pretty stingy
about doling out details before their movies

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:08.900
are released, and Avengers: Endgame is no
exception.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:13.020
However, Captain Marvel was marketed with
a completely different approach.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:17.810
There were numerous trailers, interviews,
and TV spots beforehand showing gobs of footage

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:20.430
that teased audiences to come and join in
the fun.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:24.250
The Endgame approach may be working well for
the second act to a compelling story that's

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.440
sure to bring everyone back to theaters.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.210
But the decision to provide so much information
and such a variety of teasers for Captain

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:35.250
Marvel was the right call for a film led by
such a mysterious character.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:39.460
From the sarcastic humor to shots of her glowing
from head to toe and blasting her way through

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:43.532
space, the folks at Marvel made sure that
audiences had a good sampling of the movie

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.800
before they made their decision to purchase
tickets.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:51.560
Captain Marvel heralded a huge expansion of
the MCU by properly introducing the legendary

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.280
comic book staple of the Kree-Skrull War.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.280
The movie opened with a prolonged series of
events taking place on the Kree home planet

00:04:58.280 --> 00:04:59.480
of Hala.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:04.320
Those scenes showcased the MCU's manifestation
of the Supreme Intelligence and demonstrated

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.970
just how powerful the scientifically advanced,
warlike Kree race is.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:13.780
As the movie progressed, it also introduced
everyone's favorite shapeshifters, the Skrulls.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:17.430
The pointy-eared green fellas immediately
spiced things up as they arrived on Earth

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:20.180
and shapeshifted into some bodacious surfers.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:24.940
While the Kree-Skrull conflict is an interesting
facet of the film, the real buzz for fans

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:30.000
came from the simple knowledge that the Skrulls
themselves would finally show up in the MCU.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.080
The anticipation even sparked a flood of online
theories regarding which MCU characters might

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:38.170
end up being revealed to have been a Skrull
all along.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:41.690
We know now that the Skrulls turned out to
be good guys, rather than the villains they've

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:43.420
been in the comics for years.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:47.900
But longtime comics readers' curiosity was
definitely a factor that whipped up fan interest,

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.260
and drove them to the theaters looking for
answers.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.200
Each character's story arc is interesting
in its own way.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:58.630
But perhaps one of the greatest enigmas of
the MCU thus far has been that of Nick Fury,

00:05:58.630 --> 00:05:59.840
Agent of SHIELD.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.139
Captain Marvel lured fans with its promise
to finally give him a healthy dose of backstory,

00:06:04.139 --> 00:06:05.590
and it didn't disappoint.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:10.460
We found out he'd been a soldier, an international
spy, and finally a deskbound bureaucrat before

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:12.270
he crossed paths with Danvers.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:16.440
He also apparently didn't believe in aliens,
although that changed pretty quickly once

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.110
he met up with Captain Marvel.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:22.180
We also finally found out the answer to a
question that's been haunting MCU fans for

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:26.430
so many years: how did America's Top Spy guy
lose his eye?

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:30.389
All we knew before Captain Marvel was that
Fury regarded its loss as a betrayal.

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:34.010
"The last time I trusted someone I lost an
eye."

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:36.240
Who betrayed Fury in a moment of weakness?

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.230
The cat-like Flerken named Goose, who stole
the show in Captain Marvel.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:43.890
Earlier in the film Fury suffers some damage
to his eye that ends up being nothing but

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:49.310
a red herring, but in the film's closing minutes,
Goose, who had saved his life moments earlier,

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:53.110
surprised everyone by scratching the future
SHIELD director's eye with a quick wave of

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:54.490
his alien paw.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:58.740
The betrayal clearly runs deep for the super
spy, even if he keeps Goose around during

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:00.540
the credits of Captain Marvel.

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:06.260
The 2018 box office was overshadowed by the
massive popularity of Avengers: Infinity War.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:09.950
And that makes it easy to forget that the
epic war against Thanos came on the heels

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:12.740
of the Wakandan success story Black Panther.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:17.980
The box office domination of Infinity War
is pretty easy to explain: the film had incredible

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:20.800
scope and a decade of build-up before its
release.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:25.139
However, Black Panther's similar level of
popularity came despite its smaller scope

00:07:25.139 --> 00:07:26.889
and new characters.

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:28.160
The point?

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:32.040
Marvel origin stories, especially unique ones,
attract crowds.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:36.139
T'Challa's epic was rooted outside the United
States and brought a fresh perspective to

00:07:36.139 --> 00:07:38.360
the superhero origin story.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.810
Captain Marvel, in many ways, used a similar
formula.

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:45.730
From the female lead character to the Star
Wars-esque overtones of its space-centric

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:49.930
plot, Captain Marvel combined the familiar
formula of an origin story with the thrill

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:54.680
of the unknown and did it in a universe everyone
thinks they already know so well.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:59.040
In addition to Carol Danvers' origin, it also
gave us pieces of Nick Fury's past and even

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.770
threw in a little background on fan favorites
like Phil Coulson and Ronan the Accuser.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:08.200
From lead actors to minor supporting characters,
the "origin story" element to this one was

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:09.920
about as attractive as it gets.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:14.900
Another major factor that Captain Marvel had
going for it as it entered its opening weekend

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:18.230
was that it introduced a character with an
open-ended future.

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:22.840
With over ten years of the MCU under our belts,
many of the established characters and storylines

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:24.530
may not be around much longer.

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:28.770
Thanos was teased all the way back in the
first Avengers film, while well-established

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:33.400
characters like Tony Stark and Steve Rogers
have very uncertain futures.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:36.690
This makes Carol Danvers a refreshing breath
of fresh air.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:40.320
Not only is she a new face, but she's going
to be around for a while.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:44.870
While the specific status of Brie Larson's
deal with Marvel isn't clear, she's definitely

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:46.960
on the hook for more movies to come.

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:51.300
It's a new chapter for the MCU during an era
when closure is on everyone's mind.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:56.260
Of course, when considering various reasons
that people would go to watch Captain Marvel,

00:08:56.270 --> 00:09:00.460
one of the most obvious factors that certainly
helped the film was the fact that, finally,

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:02.960
the MCU has a female lead.

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:06.650
While supporting characters like Black Widow
and Scarlet Witch have slowly been working

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:11.560
their way toward headlining projects, it was
Carol Danvers' debut that finally integrated

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:16.350
a lead female superhero into the MCU, and
audiences turned out in droves to see the

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:18.260
new superheroine step into the spotlight.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.580
"Time to show these boys how we do it.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:21.580
You ready?"

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:23.410
"Higher, further, faster, baby."

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:24.700
"That's right!"

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:30.140
The biggest reason for the overwhelming success
of Captain Marvel is… well, Marvel.

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:34.870
Carol Danvers represents part of a larger
universe that everyone can't get enough of.

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:39.820
Whether it's a crossover smash like Infinity
War, a continuation of a franchise like Thor:

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:44.490
Ragnarok, or a brand new character like Black
Panther or Captain Marvel, there's no doubt

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:47.400
at this point that Marvel has the golden touch.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:51.300
Very few studios can boast the tremendous
depth and scope of planning that Marvel has

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:52.600
brought to the table.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:57.950
Take Endgame, for example: Its plot revolves
around deeply familiar characters like Stark

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:03.200
and Banner, while simultaneously weaving in
the arrival of newcomer Captain Marvel.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.760
The complexity and quality are impressive
and exactly why everyone ponies up their cash

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:10.270
whenever a new Marvel movie hits theaters.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:14.890
On a certain level, audiences aren't really
concerned with the specifics of any one Marvel

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:17.620
film anymore, they're just going along for
the ride.

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.560
"You are home, and there is no going back.

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:26.560
No one leaves this place."

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:28.590
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:33.010
Plus, even more Looper videos about the Marvel
Cinematic Universe are coming soon.

